    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "i'm looking into some data on thermal dissipation for our high-performance testing. can you compare how ceramic vs semi-metallic brake pads handle heat soak during sustained high-speed deceleration? specifically looking at how the thermal conductivity affects the friction coefficient stability.",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Ceramic and semi-metallic brake pads handle heat soak differently, which is crucial for maintaining friction coefficient stability during sustained high-speed deceleration.  Generally, ceramic pads tend to maintain a more consistent friction coefficient across a wider temperature range because of their inherent material properties.  Semi-metallic pads, while often providing higher initial bite, can experience a more pronounced drop in friction coefficient as they reach extreme temperatures due to the composition of their friction material interacting with the heat soak.  Think of it like a slow, steady heat buildup versus a sudden, aggressive temperature spike that can cause a temporary performance dip.",
      "source": "target",
